foreign  [Music]  with analytics capability in immersive  stream for XR  you will be able to generate business  insights by understanding your customers  behaviors  in this tutorial I'm going to use a  cross-platform plugged in developed by  una media to demonstrate how to track  users interactions  I'm here with David who is the software  engineer of the plug-in and he's going  to walk you through practical use cases  David over to you hi I'm David a  software developer at unimedia and we  created a cross-platform plugin based on  the measurement protocol to track user  interactions the plugin provides an easy  solution for real-time logging of  application and game events to Google  analytics with automatic logging of key  events and user sessions and a simple  API with blueprint nodes making it easy  to log custom events and transactions  the plugin sends data using ordinary  https requests this makes it extremely  portable and available on all platforms  supported by Unreal Engine  now let's imagine a scenario where we  are an automaker and we might want to  know what customization options are the  most popular and which combinations are  frequently chosen together for  manufacturing and inventory purposes for  this example we'll be using the Auto  demo that's included with the immersive  stream for XR template project  this is a simple example of a car  configurator experience where users can  customize and preview various options  and features of the vehicle in real time  we have already modified the UI widget  to include an option for upgrading the  audio system and added a couple of  variables for keeping track of the  current configuration  now let's walk through how to make this  happen with the plugin  first we'll look at the basic setup of  the plugin the plugin is available on  the Unreal Engine Marketplace who once  purchased open the Epic Launcher and  under the Unreal Engine section click on  the library Tab and scroll down to the  Vault this is where you can access  assets purchased on the marketplace to  install simply click install to engine  then choose the desired Target engine  version the template project I'm using  is based on unreal 4.27 so I'll choose  that  with the template project open we'll go  to the plugins browser and check that  the plugin is enabled  with the plugin enabled we'll open the  project settings and navigate to the  Google analytics MP Options under the  analytics section then we'll click on  the configure Now button  this will set the plugin as the default  analytics provider now we'll need to get  the tracking ID required to send the  data we'll go to the administration  panel of our analytics account and  create a new property  a property acts as a container for the  data sent by the application  after setting up the name and other  property details we'll click on show  Advanced options and check create a  universal analytics property  for the URL you can put the associated  website if any or like in this example  use a fake URL  finally we'll select the create a  universal analytics property only option  with that setup done we can now go ahead  and create the property  you should now see a page for the  created Property displaying a tracking  ID  copy the tracking ID and head back to  unreal  now under the build configuration  settings we can enter the tracking ID  into the corresponding field for each  build setting  once you are done entering the tracking  ID you'll be prompted to restart the  editor in order to apply the settings  and that's it now you can start using  the plugin to pass data to analytics to  verify that everything is configured  correctly we'll play the application in  the editor  now back on the analytics site we'll  open the real-time report section and go  to the events page  if everything is working you should see  the app session start and app session  end events these are some automatic  events reported by the plugin and can be  disabled if not required  keep in mind that it can take a few  minutes for data to come through to the  analytics site  now that's all working let's take a look  at how to record custom events for this  we'll open the Autos menu UI widget  blueprint and add a new event to record  when a paint color is selected  first we'll create a new function to  process the current configuration and  send the event  we have already added a new variable for  keeping track of the selected paint  color so first we'll use this to get the  name of the color  next we'll add a record structured event  node this is a function node provided  with the plugin for recording events  with Analytics  we'll configure the node with a custom  string for the event category and event  action  and for the event label we'll pass in  the color name  now back in the event graph we'll add  our new function and call it whenever  one of the paint buttons is clicked  to test our event we'll click play and  change the paint color a few times  back in analytics we can see our custom  event has been recorded  note by default analytics will create a  website data view for reporting for more  relevant reporting we recommend adding a  mobile app View  to do this simply go to the admin  section  click on create View and select the  mobile app option in the new view  settings  now let's say we want to track some more  data about the car configuration such as  whether or not the user has selected the  audio system upgrade for this we'll need  to add a custom Dimension to the  property we set up on analytics with the  property selected in the administration  panel we'll click on the custom  definition section under the property  and select custom dimensions  then we'll simply create a new custom  Dimension with a relevant name  once created we'll take a note of the  assigned index number as this will be  required to associate the data with the  custom Dimension when sending the event  to Analytics  back in unreal we'll update our function  to include the audio system preference  when recording the event  to do this we'll create a new event  attribute  for the keystring we'll type in CD  followed by the index number of our  custom dimension  then for the value we'll simply pass in  a string to identify the selected audio  system option  back in the event graph we'll call our  function when the audio system selection  buttons are pressed  now we'll test our event Again by  clicking play and changing the paint and  audio system options  back in analytics we'll need to create a  custom report to see our data under the  customization section we'll go to the  custom reports page and select new  custom report  for the report type we'll select flat  table  then for the dimensions We'll add the  event label so we can see the paint  color  and our custom Dimension we set up for  the audio system  for the metrics We'll add total events  finally We'll add a filter for our event  action name and save the report  after waiting a while and adjusting the  date range we can now see our recorded  events categorized by the paint and  audio system combinations as time goes  by and more users interact with the  configurator the more data will be  collected over time with this example we  might find that a popular combination is  red paint with an audio system upgrade  Beyond this if you need Advanced query  features you can also export the data  from Google analytics to bigquery for  further analysis  that'll be it from me now back to you  jet  thank you David and that's a wrap you  can find all relevant information links  and resources in the video description  down below  I hope you find this video useful and  I'm looking forward to seeing you in the  next video

